course: dictionary
lesson: 8830
title: Shillings: old money and new
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can name a thing that existed long ago. Context helps you find out what it means. «British money then was pounds, shillings and pence: 12 pennies made a shilling and 20 shillings made a pound.» The sentence tells you a shilling is a coin, and that it is smaller than a pound.
explain: Look at the number clues. Twelve pennies made one shilling, so a shilling was worth more than a penny. Twenty shillings made one pound, so a pound was worth much more than a shilling.
explain: «Toad bargains for six shillings and sixpence» means he asks for six shillings plus one more small coin. The word "bargains" tells you that people were haggling over the price, so shillings were money used to pay.
fact: A shilling was an old British coin worth 12 old pennies.
fact: «Shillings» means more than one shilling, just as «pennies» means more than one penny.
fact: «20 shillings made a pound», so one pound was worth 240 old pennies.
fact: British money switched to decimal (100 new pence to the pound) in 1971, and the shilling stopped being used then.
fact: «The Kenyan shilling» is the money used today in Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania and Somalia, so the word still lives on in those countries.
exercise: «British money then was pounds, shillings and pence: 12 pennies made a shilling.» What was a shilling? | A paper note worth 20 pounds | *An old British coin worth 12 old pennies | A new coin worth 100 pence | A kind of bread
exercise: «Toad bargains for six shillings and sixpence and as much stew as he can eat.» How many old pennies is six shillings? | 60 | 120 | 6 | *72
exercise: Which country uses the Kenyan shilling as its money today? | Australia | Britain | Ireland | *Kenya
test: How many old pennies made one shilling? | 20 | 10 | 100 | *12
test: «20 shillings made a pound.» What did twenty shillings make? | Crown | Dollar | *Pound | Penny
exam: Which statement about the shilling is true? | *It was used in Britain until 1971, when British money became decimal | It was worth 100 pence | It is still British money today | Only Ireland has ever had it
